 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Convection is the transfer of heat through liquids and gases (fluids) by the movement of the fluid itself.\nHow it happens: the fluid near the heat becomes warm, expands and becomes less dense (lighter), so it rises. Cooler, denser fluid sinks to take its place and is heated in turn. This circular movement is a convection current.\nIn a pot of water on a fire, the water at the bottom is heated, rises, and cooler water from the top sinks; soon all the water is hot.\nConvection cannot happen in solids, because their particles cannot move from place to place.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "x": "the water at the bottom of a pot is heated"
  },
  "problem": "Describe what happens when {x}.",
  "steps": [
   "The heated water expands, becomes less dense and rises.",
   "Cooler water sinks to the bottom, is heated and rises too: a convection current forms."
  ],
  "answer": "A convection current carries the heat through all the water.",
  "checks": []
 },
